    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The Wnt signaling pathway plays an essential role in embryonic development and the regulation of many physiological processes. Defects in Wnt signaling can contribute to a variety of diseases including cancer, diabetes, and familial exudative vitreoretinopathy, among others. In β-catenin-dependent “canonical” Wnt signaling, Wnt binds to a complex of the transmembrane receptors Frizzled (Fzd) and LRP5/6, which induces recruitment of the cytoplasmic scaffold protein Dishevelled (Dvl). Very little is known about the mechanism and function of Dvl recruitment to Fzd. It is unclear whether the PDZ or DEP domains of Dvl bind Fzd, and how local membrane environment may affect Fzd-Dvl binding. A major limitation of the previous work on Fzd-Dvl interaction is the use of Fzd peptides instead of purified protein. Here, we use purified Dvl2 and Fzd4 protein to better understand how the Fzd-Dvl interaction drives Wnt signaling. We show that both purified Dvl2 DEP and PDZ bind to purified Fzd4 in fluorescence polarization studies. To our knowledge, this is the first demonstration of Fzd-Dvl binding using purified full-length Fzd protein. We also aim to determine the effects of Fzd conformational changes on Dvl recruitment. We design Fzd mutants using known familial exudative vitreoretinopathy (FEVR) mutants and a homology model of Fzd4 based on the crystal structure of full-length Smoothened. Using a cell-based signaling assay, we provide evidence that Fzd undergoes a conformational shift during Wnt activation and that this activating conformation is necessary for β-catenin signaling. The abrogation of Wnt signaling seen in Fzd with cytoplasm-facing mutations provides additional support for the existence of a cytoplasmic docking site on Frizzled. Lastly, we investigate the effect of the local lipid environment on Fzd-Dvl binding. We show that purified Dvl2 PDZ does not interact with lipid nanodiscs, and that purified Dvl2 DEP and PDZ do not interact with liposomes. Our work is an important first step in elucidating the mechanism of Fzd-Dvl recruitment and activation of downstream signaling effectors.",
